    <body>
      <sec id="article-129473.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Transrectal prostate ultrasound is a key tool for diagnosing many prostatic disorders, such as prostate cancer, prostatitis, prostatic abscess, and benign prostatic hyperplasia. Besides traditional grayscale imaging, multiple additional technologies, such as shear wave elastography, color Doppler imaging, and contrast enhancement, may aid the diagnosis.</p>
        <p>Transrectal ultrasonography is described along with the different ultrasound appearances of various prostatic pathologies. Transrectal ultrasound-guided prostate biopsies with or without&#x000a0;magnetic resonance imaging fusion guidance are reviewed in detail, as they are the primary methods of obtaining confirmatory tissue samples in patients suspected of prostate cancer.</p>
        <p>This activity outlines the indications for transrectal ultrasound evaluation of the prostate, describes the technique for transrectal ultrasound-guided prostate biopsies, outlines the role and selection of prophylactic antibiotics, summarizes the methods of local anesthesia for transrectal prostate biopsies, reviews the role of prostatic magnetic resonance imaging and magnetic resonance imaging fusion-guided prostate biopsies, compares the benefits and deficits between transrectal and transperineal biopsy procedures, and highlights the interprofessional team's role in improving care for patients with prostatic disease.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the indications for a transrectal prostate ultrasound.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Differentiate the characteristic transrectal ultrasonography findings of prostate cancer, prostatitis, prostatic abscess, and benign prostatic hyperplasia.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Determine the most common anatomical zones for prostate cancer, prostatitis, prostatic abscesses, and benign prostatic hyperplasia, and compare their findings on transrectal ultrasonography.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Communicate the role of supplemental technologies in diagnosing prostatic disease with transrectal prostate ultrasound and collaborate in performing transrectal ultrasound-guided prostate biopsies.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=129473&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=129473">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-129473.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Transrectal ultrasonography (TRUS) is a cornerstone in the evaluation of the prostate. The technique was first described in 1963 by Takahashi et al and was later used for prostate cancer detection by Gotoh and Nishi in 1965.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;However, the technique was not widely used until 1985, when 5 MHz transrectal probes became available.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r4">[4]</xref> TRUS is now commonly used for imaging prostatic pathology, especially benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH), prostate cancer, possible prostatic abscesses, and prostatitis, as well as providing visual guidance for prostate biopsies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>BPH is extremely prevalent, with 80% of men being affected by age 70.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r6">[6]</xref> Imaging is not required for diagnosis, which is typically based on clinical signs, symptom score, post-void residual urine volume, digital rectal examination (DRE) results, pressure/flow studies, and/or cystoscopy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r6">[6]</xref> However, changes in the prostate are easily recognized on TRUS, and it may be recommended for anatomical evaluation and volume measurements to help with surgical decision-making and determination of PSA density for cancer screening&#x000a0;if any surgical intervention is planned.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>Prostate cancer is the most common new cancer diagnosis in men, and the incidence is only expected to increase as the population ages.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r12">[12]</xref> The widespread worldwide routine monitoring of prostate-specific antigen (PSA) has greatly increased the overall diagnosis rate of prostate cancer.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r12">[12]</xref> However, these are mostly low-grade neoplasms frequently treated with active surveillance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r12">[12]</xref> TRUS is the most widely used imaging tool to evaluate prostate cancer, as it serves as the primary guide for prostate biopsies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;About 1 million prostate biopsies are performed in the United States and&#x000a0;3 million worldwide every year, making it one of the most commonly performed urological procedures for men.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>Prostatic abscesses appear as dark, hypoechoic cavities, frequently in the transitional zone.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;These abscesses are reliably diagnosed by TRUS, which also allows simultaneous aspiration and drainage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r15">[15]</xref></p>
        <p>Prostatitis is a fairly common disease that is classically diagnosed based on physical examination and laboratory findings alone. Still, with the more recent advancements of TRUS, imaging has played an increasing role in its diagnosis.&#x000a0;In prostatitis, the prostate appears more rounded with increased vascularity, and a periurethral halo may be observed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r16">[16]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-129473.s3" sec-type="Anatomy and Physiology">
        <title>Anatomy and Physiology</title>
        <p>The prostate is a male genitourinary organ located between the bladder and the urogenital diaphragm. This organ is shaped like a walnut and measures on average 3.75&#x000a0;to 4.0 &#x000d7; 2.5&#x000a0;to 3.0 &#x000d7; 3.1&#x000a0;to 3.8 cm (width &#x000d7; length &#x000d7; height) with a normal volume of 20&#x000a0;to 30 cc.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r17">[17]</xref> The prostatic base is adjacent to the bladder neck, with its apex at the urogenital diaphragm.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;The prostate extends from the bladder neck proximally to the membranous urethra (external sphincter) distally and wholly surrounds the prostatic urethra.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r18">[18]</xref></p>
        <p>The pubic symphysis and dorsal vein complex are anterior, the rectum is directly posterior, the pelvic side walls and levator ani are lateral, whereas the neurovascular bundles and primary arterial supply are posterolateral.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;The puboprostatic ligament supports the prostate anteriorly, and the membranous urethra provides distal stability.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r17">[17]</xref> Proximally, the prostate is contiguous with the bladder at the bladder neck, where the internal urinary sphincter is located.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;The prostate sits above the urogenital diaphragm and is surrounded by the puborectal portion of the levator ani muscle.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The seminal vesicles are paired hypoechoic sacs that join with the ampulla of the vas deferens at the posterior aspect of the prostate to form the paired ejaculatory ducts, which traverse the gland posteriorly and empty into the prostatic urethra at the verumontanum.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r22">[22]</xref> Each seminal vesicle measures<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>about 2 cm in length.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;Although they act as direct extensions, the ejaculatory ducts lack the thick muscular walls of the seminal vesicles.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r20">[20]</xref> These ejaculatory ducts are typically &#x0003c;2.4 mm in diameter unless pathologically dilated.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r20">[20]</xref></p>
        <p>The prostate is most commonly classified into anatomic zones&#x02014;the peripheral, central, transitional, and periurethral zones, in addition to the anterior fibromuscular stroma, which contains non-glandular tissue.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>The peripheral zone accounts for 70% of the prostate's mass and is where most prostate cancers arise.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r6">[6]</xref> Prostate cancer is unique in that it is typically multifocal, whereas most other malignancies tend to be unifocal unless they have metastasized.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r12">[12]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The central zone contains glandular tissue, where the ejaculatory ducts travel to empty into the posterior prostatic urethra.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;This zone is the most common for prostatitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r23">[23]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The transitional zone&#x000a0;is the smallest component and surrounds the urethra. This zone is the primary site of BPH.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r17">[17]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>These different zones are not readily differentiated on imaging. TRUS divides the gland into an inner gland (periurethral and transitional zones) and an outer gland (central, peripheral, and anterior stromal zones).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>Functionally, the prostate has a set of paired lateral lobes and a median lobe. Enlargement of these prostatic lobes by BPH&#x000a0;leads to voiding difficulties, obstructive uropathy, and a variety of lower urinary tract symptoms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;The median lobe can grow intravesically, where it can be detected by transabdominal ultrasonography. Depending on the size and shape of this median lobe extension, it may also act as a ball valve, interfering with normal micturition.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>The prostate is supplied by 3 branches of the internal iliac arteries&#x02014;the internal pudendal, inferior vesicle, and middle rectal arteries.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r17">[17]</xref> The prostate receives venous blood from the dorsal vein of the penis and drains into the iliac veins. There is also a communication to the internal vertebral venous plexus (Batson plexus), which accounts for the metastatic spread of prostate cancer to the vertebral bodies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;The lymphatic drainage is primarily to the internal iliac and sacral lymph nodes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;This drainage receives parasympathetic supply from pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-4) and sympathetic supply from the inferior hypogastric plexuses.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r17">[17]</xref> The neurovascular bundle travels posterolateral to the prostate.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <p>The primary function of the prostate is reproductive, as it produces a secretion that constitutes about 25% of the semen. The glandular prostatic tissue produces the glycoprotein PSA, which liquefies semen, citrate, multiple prostaglandins (A, E, and F), prostatic acid phosphatase, spermidine, spermine, and zinc.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r24">[24]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r25">[25]</xref>&#x000a0;All of these are important in protecting and mobilizing sperm.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r24">[24]</xref></p>
        <p>Prostatic secretions are alkaline, which is necessary to neutralize the vagina's normal acidity, which otherwise damages the sperm.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r26">[26]</xref>&#x000a0;The prostate gland is also the site of the intracellular conversion of testosterone into dihydrotestosterone, utilizing the enzyme 5&#x003b1;-reductase.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r21">[21]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-129473.s4" sec-type="Indications">
        <title>Indications</title>
        <p>TRUS-guided prostate biopsies&#x000a0;are frequently used for patients suspected of having prostate cancer due to hard nodules on DRE or persistently elevated PSA levels.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r28">[28]</xref>&#x000a0;Any patient with unexplained, persistently elevated PSA levels; a hard, suspicious nodule on DRE; or hypoechoic areas on transrectal prostatic ultrasound, especially in the peripheral zone, should undergo a prostatic biopsy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r28">[28]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r30">[30]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r31">[31]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r32">[32]</xref> Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/27834">Prostate Cancer Screening</ext-link>,"&#x000a0;for further information.</p>
        <p>Although there are many indications, TRUS is most often used for guidance in prostatic biopsies, aquablation therapy for BPH, prostatic brachytherapy, and placement of fiduciary markers before radiation therapy for prostate cancer.</p>
        <p>Indications for TRUS of the prostate include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Aquabation or similar ultrasound-guided therapy for BPH</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Estimation of prostate size when treatment is needed for BPH</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Evaluation and aspiration of suspected prostatic abscesses</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Evaluation for an unexplained, persistently elevated PSA or a hard prostatic nodule suspicious for prostatic cancer on DRE</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Evaluation of selected cases of persistent hematospermia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>High-intensity focused ultrasound treatment of prostate cancer or BPH</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Male infertility</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Placement of fiduciary markers before external beam radiation therapy for prostate cancer</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Prostatic brachytherapy for cancer</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Prostatic cryotherapy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Transrectal or transperineal prostate biopsy&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r33">[33]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-129473.s5" sec-type="Contraindications">
        <title>Contraindications</title>
        <p>There are no prostatic conditions that constitute absolute contraindications to TRUS. However, probe insertion may be precluded by unrelated anorectal pathologies, including rectal masses, malignancies, hemorrhoids, anal fissures, dermatologic lesions, prior rectal surgery, or strictures. These factors are typically evaluated during the pre-procedural DRE.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r22">[22]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-129473.s6" sec-type="Equipment">
        <title>Equipment</title>
        <p>This technique uses endorectal linear or convex transducers with high frequencies (7.5-10 MHz is acceptable; however, higher-frequency transducers are preferred for better resolution).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r34">[34]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r35">[35]</xref> Both single-plane and biplane transducers may be used; however, biplane transducers are preferred, as they do not require exchanging probes to view both axial and sagittal planes necessary for a complete evaluation, thereby decreasing the patient's overall discomfort.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r34">[34]</xref> The ability to simultaneously view both sagittal imaging with the projected biopsy need track and axial imaging for spatial orientation is a significant advantage of using biplanar transducers.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r34">[34]</xref> Images are obtained using B-mode (brightness) imaging.</p>
        <p>Several supplemental tools may also be utilized for evaluation that vary in availability based on the institution and available equipment:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Color and power Doppler are widely available tools for assessing vascularity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r36">[36]</xref> These tools are particularly useful for evaluating inflammatory conditions, blood flow, or overall tissue vascularity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r36">[36]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r37">[37]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Elastography is an emerging technology that helps identify differences in tissue stiffness, such as in the relatively stiff prostate tissue associated with prostate cancer.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r38">[38]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Three-dimensional ultrasonography provides a more detailed view of the prostate, allowing evaluation in the coronal plane and clarifying the spatial relationships of the prostatic capsule and adjacent structures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r39">[39]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r40">[40]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Contrast-enhanced ultrasound uses microbubble ultrasonographic contrast agents to better demonstrate tissue enhancement and neovascularity with increased vascular density generally associated with malignancy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r41">[41]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r42">[42]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r43">[43]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r44">[44]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>If the ultrasound is being&#x000a0;performed to guide a prostate biopsy, the preferred probe is a biplanar transducer with axial and sagittal imaging capabilities. Being able to simultaneously&#x000a0;view the axial plane (for right and left orientation) and the sagittal without changing transducers is highly advantageous. The probe must contain an internal bioptic channel or attached biopsy needle guide, corresponding to the needle track demonstrated on the ultrasound screen in sagittal mode.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r33">[33]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r45">[45]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-129473.s7" sec-type="Personnel">
        <title>Personnel</title>
        <p>For urologists, proficiency in TRUS is attained during residency training or by attending appropriate postgraduate hands-on instructional courses.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>When performing TRUS-guided biopsies, a trained assistant is essential. The assistant's responsibilities include removing each specimen after collection, placing it in the appropriately labeled container, sterilizing the needle tip by briefly dipping it in formalin (optional but recommended), re-cocking the biopsy gun, resetting the safety, and carefully handing the device back to the urologist.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-129473.s8" sec-type="Preparation">
        <title>Preparation</title>
        <p>Depending on the institution, a suppository or enema may be ordered before the ultrasound to ensure that the rectum is empty.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r46">[46]</xref> However, this is somewhat controversial if a transrectal biopsy is not being planned.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r46">[46]</xref>&#x000a0;Nevertheless, a simple mechanical cleansing of the rectum immediately before TRUS seems reasonable to reduce artifacts, improve imaging, evacuate feces, eliminate gas, and reduce the risk of infection with minimal risk or significant patient discomfort.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r47">[47]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r48">[48]</xref></p>
        <p>Risk factors for post-procedural infectious complications include bacterial prostatitis, diabetes, a history of urinary tract infections or urinary retention, indwelling catheterization, lower urinary tract symptoms, obesity (body mass index &#x0003e;28), and previous antibiotic use.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r49">[49]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r50">[50]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r51">[51]</xref></p>
        <p>Before inserting the ultrasound probe, a digital rectal exam must be performed to assess for any possible contraindications or potential difficulties.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r33">[33]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Prophylactic Antibiotic Management,&#x000a0; Selection, and Preoperative Patient Preparation for Transurethral Ultrasound-Guided Prostate Biopsies</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>The patient is typically instructed to take an oral antibiotic before the procedure, possibly starting a full day prior, or may be given an intramuscular antibiotic injection in the clinic at the time of the biopsy. These measures significantly reduce the risk of a post-procedure infection and are highly recommended.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r52">[52]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r53">[53]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r54">[54]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Post-procedure, prophylactic antibiotics may be continued for 1 or more days. (1-3 days is typical.)<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r55">[55]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Fluoroquinolones are the most commonly used antibiotics for prophylaxis, but growing bacterial resistance is a major concern worldwide.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r56">[56]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r57">[57]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r58">[58]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r59">[59]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r60">[60]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r61">[61]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r62">[62]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r63">[63]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>As a general guide, if more than 20% of local <italic toggle="yes">Escherichia coli</italic> strains are resistant to fluoroquinolones, a different class of antibiotics is suggested for prophylaxis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r52">[52]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The recent administration of fluoroquinolones or third-generation cephalosporins increases the risk of resistance to these antibiotics when used for prophylaxis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r64">[64]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Targeted antibiotic therapy guided by rectal swab cultures is encouraged as it has been shown to reduce post-procedure infectious complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r65">[65]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r66">[66]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r67">[67]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r68">[68]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>A full day of preoperative antibiotics and targeted antibiotic therapy is suggested in areas with high fluoroquinolone resistance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r56">[56]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r69">[69]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The preferred antibiotic alternative to fluoroquinolones may well be fosfomycin.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r56">[56]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r70">[70]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r71">[71]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Fosfomycin has many favorable beneficial qualities:<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r56">[56]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r57">[57]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r58">[58]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r70">[70]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r71">[71]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r72">[72]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r73">[73]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r74">[74]</xref>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Good prostatic tissue penetration after a single 3-g oral dose.</p></list-item><list-item><p>A single dose, 1 hour preoperatively, was just as effective as multiple doses.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r75">[75]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Bacterial resistance is low.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Fosfomycin is effective against many highly drug-resistant organisms.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Fosfomycin&#x000a0;is readily available and has&#x000a0;an excellent safety profile.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Efficacy is improved when fosfomycin is taken together with a fluoroquinolone.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r76">[76]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Alternative prophylactic antibiotics that can be used alone or in combination include aztreonam, amikacin, ceftriaxone, ceftriaxone + gentamicin, metronidazole, cefuroxime, and cefpodoxime.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>There is no consensus on the best combination, so antibiotic selection depends on local bacterial resistance patterns, physician preference, institutional guidelines, and patient clinical characteristics.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r55">[55]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r77">[77]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Targeted antibiotics (based on rectal swab cultures before the biopsy) are suggested when feasible, but especially for high-risk patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r65">[65]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r66">[66]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r78">[78]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r79">[79]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r80">[80]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r81">[81]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r82">[82]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r83">[83]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Targeted preoperative antibiotics can reduce postoperative infections by about 42% compared to empiric antibiotics.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r84">[84]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>A Fleet enema for mechanical rectal cleansing is somewhat controversial but is generally recommended about 2 hours before the procedure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r47">[47]</xref>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>The Fleet enema optimizes ultrasound transmission, enhances visualization of the prostate, reduces the risk of infection for biopsies, and has no adverse effects.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r47">[47]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Additional rectal antimicrobial preparation with a povidone-iodine antiseptic solution, swabs, or gel has also been recommended immediately before transrectal biopsies, as it has shown efficacy in reducing postoperative infections and sepsis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r85">[85]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r86">[86]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r87">[87]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r88">[88]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r89">[89]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r90">[90]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>A transperineal approach is strongly preferred for patients with a high risk of infection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r91">[91]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-129473.s9" sec-type="Technique or Treatment">
        <title>Technique or Treatment</title>
        <p>
<bold>Transabdominal Ultrasonog</bold>r<bold>aphy</bold></p>
        <p>In male patients, the prostate gland may be partially visualized transabdominally, particularly when the bladder is full. The prostate is just distal and inferior to the bladder and posterior to the pubic symphysis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r17">[17]</xref> Sonographic evaluation can provide a general estimate of prostate size and contour, aiding in the assessment of prostatomegaly or bladder outlet obstruction.</p>
        <p>Although not as detailed as the transrectal approach, transabdominal ultrasonography offers a noninvasive, reproducible, and readily accessible method to screen for prostatic enlargement in symptomatic patients and estimate the prostate's volume.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r92">[92]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r93">[93]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Transrectal Ultrasonography</bold>
</p>
        <p>Biplanar high-frequency (7.5-10 MHz) endorectal linear or curved array transducers are generally used.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r46">[46]</xref>&#x000a0;Biplanar transducers enable simultaneous sagittal and transverse views without requiring probe switching, facilitating visualization for transrectal prostatic biopsies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r46">[46]</xref>&#x000a0;Higher-frequency probes are&#x000a0;typically&#x000a0;preferred due to their improved resolution.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r46">[46]</xref></p>
        <p>The patient is placed in the lateral decubitus position, most commonly on their left side, with their knees flexed to their chest. The lithotomy position may also be used; however, patients generally tolerate the left lateral decubitus position better.</p>
        <p>The transducer is covered with ultrasound gel and placed in a condom. Air bubbles are removed, and the condom-covered probe is now well-lubricated externally.&#x000a0;Any rectal analgesia or antiseptic may now be applied.&#x000a0;Immediately before the ultrasound probe is inserted, a DRE should be performed to assess for any possible contraindications or difficulties and to help indicate the optimal angle of probe insertion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r33">[33]</xref></p>
        <p>After the DRE, the transducer is inserted with the ultrasound directed anteriorly. The minimal amount of pressure possible should be applied to the transducer probe to minimize patient discomfort and avoid altering the contour of the gland. If resistance is encountered, the angle of the transducer may be adjusted accordingly.</p>
        <p>Once the anal sphincter is traversed, the transducer typically advances with much greater ease.</p>
        <p>Scanning is typically performed first in the transverse plane, from the base to the apex. In this plane, the symmetry and shape are assessed, as well as the width of the gland and the associated seminal vesicles. Once the gland is evaluated in the transverse axis, either the probe is removed (if it is a single-planar probe) or the axis is switched (if it is a biplanar probe). The prostate is then evaluated in the sagittal plane, assessing the height and length as well as the seminal vesicles and prostatic urethra.</p>
        <p>Overall echogenicity (focal and diffuse) and changes in echogenicity are primary considerations in the evaluation. The magnification and gain should be adjusted to achieve optimal imaging, ensuring the prostate fills the screen and the peripheral zone appears as a medium gray. Additionally, if clinically warranted, the bladder may be assessed before and after voiding to evaluate for post-void residual volume.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r33">[33]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r94">[94]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r95">[95]</xref></p>
        <p>Throughout the examination, multiple additional features may be used to assess the prostate further, as described below:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Color and power Doppler may be applied to any grayscale image.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r36">[36]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r96">[96]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r97">[97]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r98">[98]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r99">[99]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Elastography measures tissue stiffness, which is increased in cancerous tissue. There are 2 different ultrasound elastography techniques:<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r98">[98]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r99">[99]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r100">[100]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r101">[101]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <list list-type="bullet">
              <list-item>
                <p>Compression elastography measures the amount of displacement after repetitive compression and decompression while scanning the prostate.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r100">[100]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r102">[102]</xref></p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Shear wave elastography produces shear waves in the tissue and measures the wave velocity. Shear wave velocity is directly associated with tissue stiffness as the waves move faster through denser, stiffer tissue.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r103">[103]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r104">[104]</xref>&#x000a0;Images require avoiding placing any pressure on the prostate.</p>
              </list-item>
            </list>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Contrast enhancement uses injected microbubble contrast agents that harmlessly pass through the pulmonary vasculature due to their small size (&#x0003c;1 &#x000b5;m), thereby enhancing the visualization of end organs, such as&#x000a0;the prostate.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r98">[98]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r99">[99]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r105">[105]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r106">[106]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r107">[107]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r108">[108]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r109">[109]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r110">[110]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r111">[111]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r112">[112]</xref>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Enhancement after bolus injection typically lasts only 1&#x000a0;to 2 minutes; slow intravenous infusion can extend this enhancement.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Imaging is usually performed with Doppler or grayscale harmonic settings. However, newer techniques (intermittent harmonic imaging or flash-replenishment technique) may also allow for increased contrast penetration into the prostate.
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Intermittent harmonic imaging uses a slower frame rate, thereby prolonging microbubble survival and allowing for better visualization of the microvascularity associated with prostate cancer.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r45">[45]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r110">[110]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r112">[112]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r113">[113]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r114">[114]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Flash replenishment uses high-power ultrasonography to destroy the microbubbles, followed by low-power pulses to visualize vascular replenishment. This imaging technique provides detailed visualization of the prostatic vasculature, enabling the accurate targeting of biopsies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r45">[45]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r115">[115]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r116">[116]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Three-dimensional images are obtained by slowly sliding the transrectal probe through the entire prostate, from apex to base, using axial imaging. The data are saved as voxels and can be viewed in any plane for optimal assessment and visualization of spatial relationships.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r33">[33]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r45">[45]</xref>&#x000a0;This imaging technique is commonly used for magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) fusion TRUS-guided prostate biopsies.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Transrectal Ultrasonography of Prostatic Pathology</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Benign prostatic hyperplasia:</bold> BPH is a common ultrasound finding and&#x000a0;is identified in most men aged 50 or older. BPH presents as an asymmetrical enlargement or multiple hypoechoic nodules in the transition zone and periurethral glands.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r46">[46]</xref> Over time, this may progress to a more homogenous echogenicity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r27">[27]</xref> There may be deviation of the urethra and intrusion of the prostate into the bladder.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r27">[27]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Typical TRUS changes in BPH include an increased resistive index, enlargement of the transitional zone with multiple hypoechoic areas, echogenic (compressed) peripheral and central zones that create a surgical capsule, transitional zone calcifications, and a larger overall prostate size.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r34">[34]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r117">[117]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r118">[118]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r119">[119]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r120">[120]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r121">[121]</xref>&#x000a0;Smoothed wall adenomas with decreased internal vascularity are also a common ultrasonic finding in BPH.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r27">[27]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Due to increased intraprostatic pressure, prostatic enlargement increases vascular resistance, raising the resistive index.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r121">[121]</xref>&#x000a0;Vascular resistance and the resistive index also increase as the transitional zone enlarges and compresses the peripheral zone tissue against the capsule.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r121">[121]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>TRUS can be used to estimate prostatic size before surgical procedures for BPH and to identify the intravesical extension of the median lobe into the bladder.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r122">[122]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r123">[123]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r124">[124]</xref>&#x000a0;An enlarged prostate is diagnosed when the size of the gland is greater than 20&#x000a0;to 30 cc.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r46">[46]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r125">[125]</xref>&#x000a0;The prostatic volume is typically calculated by measuring the length, height, and width, and then multiplying by the ellipsoidal coefficient of &#x003c0;/6 (or 0.52).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r122">[122]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r123">[123]</xref>&#x000a0;However, this formula underestimates the prostatic volume by 10% to 18% in most cases (80%), especially in larger prostates greater than 60 g.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r95">[95]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r122">[122]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r126">[126]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>A revised formula using a coefficient factor of 0.66 instead of the traditional ellipsoidal coefficient of 0.52 has proven superior in more reliably estimating prostatic volumes. Still, it has not yet become the accepted clinical standard.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r122">[122]</xref> This&#x000a0;approach is very similar to the proposed bullet formula, which uses a coefficient factor of 0.65.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r95">[95]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>PSA density: </bold>The PSA density is determined by dividing the PSA value by the prostatic volume.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r127">[127]</xref> A higher PSA density suggests prostatic malignancy; lower values indicate BPH.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r127">[127]</xref>&#x000a0;An abnormal PSA density, as defined by international consensus, is generally considered to be any value greater than 0.15 ng/mL/cc. However, higher cutoff values, such as&#x02265;0.18 ng/mL/cc,&#x000a0;&#x02265;0.20&#x000a0;ng/mL/cc, and even 0.30 ng/mL/cc, have been suggested by various studies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r128">[128]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r129">[129]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r130">[130]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r131">[131]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r132">[132]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r133">[133]</xref>&#x000a0;The PSA density is a useful parameter, but it is not definitive. The PSA density is best used in conjunction with physical examination findings, clinical symptoms, imaging results, bioassay risk stratification tests, and other relevant data to determine the likelihood of malignancy and whether a prostatic biopsy should be performed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r134">[134]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Prostate cancer: </bold>Prostate cancer typically appears as a hypoechoic lesion in the outer gland (peripheral zone) on TRUS and is often multifocal. However, the appearance varies, as it can also appear isoechoic, complicating identification. Therefore, additional techniques&#x000a0;are necessary for diagnosis.&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Color or power Doppler may show increased blood flow; however, this is not a highly sensitive method and is typically observed only in higher-grade cancers.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r36">[36]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r98">[98]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r99">[99]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Contrast-enhanced ultrasonography can reveal increased blood flow due to the tumor's higher microvessel density. Because of increased cell density and stiffness, prostate cancer exhibits decreased elasticity, aiding in diagnosis and guiding biopsies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r98">[98]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r107">[107]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r108">[108]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Grayscale imaging can evaluate tumor extent and may show bulges, irregularities of the prostatic contour, and disrupted periprostatic fat.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>A hyperechoic seminal vesicle, asymmetric enlargement, or anterior displacement (altered prostate-seminal vesicle angle) suggests seminal vesicle involvement.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Prostatic MRI provides superior differentiation of suspicious prostatic nodules from benign tissue and is preferred over ultrasound for diagnostic cancer imaging, evaluation of extracapsular extension, and assessment of regional lymph node involvement.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The presence of extracapsular extension can also be assessed using 3-dimensional ultrasonography, which is an alternative to cross-sectional imaging, to demonstrate spatial relationships more effectively. An important area to evaluate is the relationship of any detected cancer to the neurovascular bundle, which travels along the posterolateral aspect of the prostate. If the cancer spares this region, it allows for the possibility of anerve-sparing radical prostatectomy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r22">[22]</xref></p>
        <p>TRUS has not been considered a particularly reliable diagnostic imaging modality for prostate cancer, although it is beneficial for guiding prostatic biopsies. Hypoechoic lesions have classically been associated with prostate malignancies. Still, up to 40% of prostate cancers are isoechoic, and large recent series have not indicated a substantially increased detection rate of cancer in hypoechoic nodules.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r135">[135]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r136">[136]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r137">[137]</xref></p>
        <p>Current guidelines recommend that all hypoechoic lesions in the peripheral zone of the prostate on transrectal ultrasonography should be biopsied.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r30">[30]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r31">[31]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r32">[32]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Prostatic abscesses: </bold>These&#x000a0;abscesses&#x000a0;are best visualized by TRUS, which provides an overall diagnostic accuracy of at least 80% for these lesions and offers the opportunity for guided needle aspiration.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r15">[15]</xref> On ultrasound, prostatic abscesses appear as dark, fluid-filled hypoechoic intraprostatic cavities, which often contain thick pus.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;The lesion may be spherical or oblong and may be surrounded by a dark, hypoechoic halo, with&#x000a0;bright, hyperechoic debris.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;Prostatic abscesses typically develop in the transitional zone.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>TRUS can also guide needle aspiration of these cavities for culture, drainage, and lavage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;This technique can also be used to monitor the progress of the abscess after drainage or aspiration.&#x000a0;Although TRUS is the preferred imaging method for prostatic abscesses, computed tomography (CT) scans or MRIs may be alternatives.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Prostatic cysts:</bold>&#x000a0;These cysts&#x000a0;are often detected on prostatic ultrasonography. These cysts become clinically significant if they interfere with male fertility. Congenital cysts may originate from either M&#x000fc;llerian or Wolffian ducts. Persistent M&#x000fc;llerian duct cysts appear as midline, anechoic cystic structures with a teardrop shape when viewed in the sagittal plane. These cysts are often associated with hypospadias (11% of hypospadias patients), but can be as high as 40% in patients with posterior hypospadias.<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/Keywords/UserViewPopup/178762#link_26060552">[79]</ext-link> The incidence of M&#x000fc;llerian duct cysts increases with higher grades of hypospadias, as the urethral meatus becomes more proximal or posterior.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r138">[138]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r139">[139]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r140">[140]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r141">[141]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r142">[142]</xref></p>
        <p>Uncommon hypoechoic prostatic lesions include granulomas, hematomas, infarcts, and lymphomas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r30">[30]</xref> Cysts of the vas deferens and seminal vesicles may be associated with renal agenesis and cystic kidney disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r143">[143]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r144">[144]</xref>&#x000a0;Prolonged hypogonadism can make the prostate appear hypoplastic.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Prostatitis: </bold>Prostatitis&#x000a0;often causes the normally ovoid prostate to appear more rounded with decreased echogenicity due to increased edema. Small hypoechoic areas may be observed, indicating early small abscesses, and calcifications or fibrosis may be visible in the apical peripheral zone. Color Doppler&#x000a0;typically shows increased vascularity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;Dystrophic calcifications or fibrosis in the apical peripheral zone can also be observed. A periurethral hypoechoic halo is characteristic of prostatitis but can sometimes be mistaken for the periprostatic sphincter, which is also hypoechoic.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r16">[16]</xref> TRUS is also used selectively in chronic bacterial prostatitis that has not responded to standard therapy, and intraprostatic injections of antibiotics are indicated.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r21">[21]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Artifacts in transrectal prostatic ultrasonography: </bold>This technique can affect the image quality and analysis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r36">[36]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r145">[145]</xref> Common examples include the following:<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r36">[36]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r145">[145]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r146">[146]</xref></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Blooming occurs when color from an area of blood flow enhancement extends into adjacent tissues on color Doppler imaging, which is typically caused by setting the color gain too high.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>False positives&#x000a0;for malignancy can occur due to alterations in beam thickness, attenuation, or refraction.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Narrow or incomplete fields of view&#x000a0;are caused by an inaccurate setting for prostatic volume, reverberation or shadowing artifacts, or mirror imaging.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Overly complex echo patterns&#x000a0;may be caused by artifacts from rectal balloon reflections, mirror-imaging, or side lobes.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pseudomasses can arise from imaging rectal folds or reflections from an intraluminal fluid level, resulting in a mirror image.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Size artifacts are produced by lesions too small to characterize otherwise.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Transrectal Ultrasound-Guided Biopsy&#x000a0;of&#x000a0;the Prostate</bold>
</p>
        <p>Although&#x000a0;prostatic biopsies are typically performed using the biopsy channel of the transrectal probe, they can also be performed with a transperineal approach while still using TRUS for guidance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r147">[147]</xref> This approach significantly reduces the risk of infection and can still be performed in a clinician's office or clinic. A dorsal lithotomy position is used. This approach is particularly suited for patients at high risk for infection. Antibiotic prophylaxis may not be necessary with a perineal biopsy approach.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r148">[148]</xref></p>
        <p>The patient should be instructed to discontinue taking blood thinners appropriately before the procedure and to use a Fleet enema 2 hours before the biopsy.</p>
        <p>The following technique is employed for transrectal ultrasonographic examinations of the prostate and ultrasound-guided prostatic biopsies:<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r28">[28]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r149">[149]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r150">[150]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Patient positioning and probe insertion:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Optimal patient positioning is the left lateral decubitus position.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r47">[47]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The buttocks should be aligned with the side of the table or just beyond it, and both legs should be flexed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r47">[47]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The lithotomy position may also be used, but has been associated with greater patient discomfort.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r151">[151]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Rectal anesthetic and povidone-iodine may be applied just before probe insertion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r85">[85]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r152">[152]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r153">[153]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r154">[154]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r155">[155]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r156">[156]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r157">[157]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Biplanar transrectal probes are preferred and strongly recommended.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r47">[47]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The probe is covered in ultrasound gel, inserted into a condom, and any air bubbles are eliminated.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The condom cover with the probe inside is coated generously with additional ultrasonic gel.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The additional use of&#x000a0;intrarectal local anesthesia, such as lidocaine-prilocaine cream or lidocaine gel, further improves pain control during TRUS examinations and biopsies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r156">[156]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r158">[158]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r159">[159]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r160">[160]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r161">[161]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Data suggest that heated lidocaine gel (to 40 &#x000b0;C) provides superior rectal analgesia for probe insertion and manipulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r162">[162]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r163">[163]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r164">[164]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>A DRE should be performed immediately before probe insertion to identify any pathology or potential technical problems and to determine the optimal angle for probe insertion.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The lidocaine gel can be spread and massaged into the mucosa during the DRE.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r165">[165]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The condom-covered, lubricated ultrasonic probe is slowly and gently inserted into the rectum, tilted slightly anteriorly, and advanced until the prostate can be visualized on axial imaging.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Using sterile ultrasound gel is essential, as infections have been reported with multi-use gel dispensers that have become contaminated.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r166">[166]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>B (brightness) mode ultrasound imaging is used. (B-mode is grayscale imaging that uses each dot's brightness to represent the returning echo's strength. Denser tissues reflect more echoes and give a brighter, more echogenic image.)<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r167">[167]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Diagnostic examination of the prostate:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>After adjusting the ultrasound parameters, including optimal sizing, field of view, resolution, gain, and overall visualization, the prostate is measured in height, width, and length, and its volume is subsequently calculated.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The gland is then systematically examined.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r47">[47]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The axial view is often used first, starting at the base.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The transducer is then slowly moved toward the apex.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Any abnormalities or lesions are meticulously documented, including relevant characteristics such as size, echogenicity, homogeneity, laterality, and anatomical location.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r47">[47]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>These abnormalities include hypoechoic and hyperechoic nodules, calcifications, contour alterations, and cystic structures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r47">[47]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>A similar examination is performed using the sagittal plane.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The seminal vesicles should be examined for evidence of tumor invasion, such as unexplained dilation, unusual echogenicity, asymmetry, or abnormal angulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r47">[47]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The area of the neurovascular bundles should be carefully examined for signs of invasion that could affect surgical decision-making later.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Periprostatic nerve block and prostatic anesthesia</bold>
</p>
        <p>TRUS-guided prostate biopsies require a periprostatic block to anesthetize the neurovascular bundles containing the nerves supplying sensory innervation to the prostate, as well as a separate injection of local anesthetic to the prostatic apex.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r168">[168]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r169">[169]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r170">[170]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r171">[171]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r172">[172]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r173">[173]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r174">[174]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r175">[175]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r176">[176]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r177">[177]</xref></p>
        <p>Above the dentate line of the anal canal, sensation is primarily splanchnic innervation with a relatively insensitive mucosa.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r178">[178]</xref> Pain is well-controlled with periprostatic anesthesia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r171">[171]</xref> However, the tissues below the dentate line are extremely sensitive to pain due to somatic innervation via the inferior rectal nerve, so a separate local anesthetic injection to the prostatic apex is recommended.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r170">[170]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r176">[176]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r179">[179]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r180">[180]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r181">[181]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r182">[182]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r183">[183]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r184">[184]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Several large meta-analyses have demonstrated the effectiveness of this combination of bilateral periprostatic and apical injections for optimal pain control during transrectal prostatic biopsies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r170">[170]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r171">[171]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r172">[172]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r173">[173]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r174">[174]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r175">[175]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r176">[176]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r177">[177]</xref>&#x000a0;Rectal lidocaine gel (heated, if possible) appears to be beneficial in further reducing discomfort associated with probe insertion and subsequent manipulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r162">[162]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r163">[163]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r164">[164]</xref></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Using TRUS, a triangular hyperechoic fatty area (called the Mount Everest sign) can be identified at 5 and 7 o'clock on either side of the prostate at the junction between the seminal vesicle, rectum, and the prostatic base on transrectal longitudinal sagittal imaging.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r165">[165]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r179">[179]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r180">[180]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r181">[181]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r182">[182]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Approximately 5 to 10 mL of 1%&#x000a0;to 2% lidocaine is injected into each of these triangular areas using a long (22 gauge, 7 inch) spinal needle passed through the biopsy channel of the TRUS probe and monitored using ultrasound.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r179">[179]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r180">[180]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r181">[181]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r182">[182]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Observing an appropriately sized hypoechoic wheal on ultrasound in the correct location confirms an adequate anesthetic injection.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Local anesthetic should also be administered to the prostatic apex, between the prostate, rectum, and the puborectalis muscles, as this area contains sensitive somatic nerves that can cause pain despite the earlier periprostatic block.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r170">[170]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r179">[179]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r180">[180]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r181">[181]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r182">[182]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r183">[183]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r185">[185]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r186">[186]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r187">[187]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r188">[188]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Evidence indicates that a direct injection into the prostate offers additional analgesic benefit, but there is a risk of increased systemic lidocaine absorption or inadvertent intravascular administration.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r156">[156]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r170">[170]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r183">[183]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r189">[189]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r190">[190]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r191">[191]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r192">[192]</xref> Therefore, such injections should be administered with caution.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The additional use of&#x000a0;intrarectal local anesthesia, such as lidocaine-prilocaine cream or lidocaine gel, further improves pain control during TRUS-guided examinations and biopsies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r156">[156]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r158">[158]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r159">[159]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r160">[160]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r161">[161]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r193">[193]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Using heated lidocaine gel (40 &#x000b0;C) provides superior rectal analgesia for transducer insertion and manipulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r149">[149]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r162">[162]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r163">[163]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r164">[164]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The anesthetic block takes about 5 to 10 minutes to be fully effective.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The details of local anesthetic administration for transperineal prostate biopsies are well described elsewhere.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r165">[165]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Biopsy Technique</bold>
</p>
        <p>Sampling the apex of the prostate is essential, as it consists of peripheral zone tissue, is a frequent location for finding malignancy, and is the most common site for missed cancers on TRUS-guided biopsies. This region is sometimes overlooked due to the pain it causes unless specifically anesthetized.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r170">[170]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r179">[179]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r194">[194]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r195">[195]</xref>&#x000a0;The standard technique is described as follows:<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r28">[28]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r150">[150]</xref></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Biopsies are typically taken with an 18-gauge needle using a spring-operated biopsy gun. The needle is long enough to pass through the biopsy channel of the transrectal probe and into the prostate.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The biopsy needle is easily visible on ultrasound, and its expected path can be outlined as a dotted line on the sagittal view of most modern ultrasound machines.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Simultaneous axial and sagittal viewing makes the biopsy technically easier and ensures that both sides are sampled.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>When activated, the biopsy gun advances the needle 0.5 cm and samples the next 1.5 cm of prostatic tissue immediately proximal to that point.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r196">[196]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>After taking the biopsy, the biopsy gun is removed from the probe and handed to an assistant, who then removes the sample, labels it, dips the tip in antiseptic (if applicable), resets the spring, turns the safety on, and returns it carefully to the surgeon.
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Dipping the end of the biopsy needle in formalin between taking samples disinfects the needle tip, helping reduce the risk of infection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r197">[197]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r198">[198]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Alcohol is also used to disinfect the tip of the biopsy needle between samples and appears efficacious.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r199">[199]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r200">[200]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Chlorhexidine can likewise be used and appears superior to formalin when disinfecting the biopsy needle tip between biopsy samples.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r86">[86]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Standard prostate biopsies typically include about 12 samples.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r201">[201]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r202">[202]</xref>&#x000a0;This number is a reasonable compromise between obtaining adequate prostate sampling, post-biopsy complication rate, infection risk, and patient discomfort.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r47">[47]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r201">[201]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r202">[202]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r203">[203]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>A common approach is to obtain two samples each from the base, mid-gland, and apex of both lobes, yielding 12 biopsy cores placed in 6 specimen jars.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r204">[204]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Repeated sampling of the same site should be avoided; for subsequent passes, the needle should be redirected slightly to a different location.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Taking additional biopsy samples in patients with larger prostates appears reasonable.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r205">[205]</xref>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Extra lateral core samples increase the cancer detection rate.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r206">[206]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Increasing the number of samples from 12 to 18 in prostates weighing more than 55 g has been suggested.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r205">[205]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Additional samples from the transition zone do not increase the cancer detection rate.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r47">[47]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r207">[207]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Routine biopsies of the seminal vesicles are not indicated.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r47">[47]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r207">[207]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>For better localization, biopsy samples from different regions of the prostate should be submitted in separate containers. A standard procedure uses six labeled containers&#x02014;one each for the base, mid-gland, and apex on both the right and left sides&#x02014;commonly referred to as a sextant biopsy. Each container should contain at least&#x000a0;2 biopsy cores.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Biopsy samples should preferentially be taken from the lateral aspects of the prostate with adequate sampling of the apex.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r206">[206]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r208">[208]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r209">[209]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r210">[210]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Any identified hypoechoic areas, particularly from the peripheral zone, should also be biopsied.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r30">[30]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r31">[31]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r32">[32]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r47">[47]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Saturation biopsies (typically about 25 core samples) or MRI fusion-guided biopsies are recommended when initial standard biopsies have been negative, but there remains a significant suspicion of an underlying malignancy.]<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r211">[211]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r212">[212]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r213">[213]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r214">[214]</xref>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>These procedures are best performed with MRI fusion technology and usually require regional or general anesthesia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r215">[215]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Saturation biopsies are inappropriate for initial prostatic biopsies, but MRI fusion-guided biopsies are recommended.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r47">[47]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>After the biopsy procedure, the probe is slowly and gently withdrawn, and a small gauze pad, pack, or dressing is applied to the rectum.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The patient is told to sit up carefully and observed for a short period, usually less than 30 minutes, with several vital signs readings periodically to monitor for abnormal changes and excessive rectal bleeding.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The patient is told to expect some rectal and urinary bleeding.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The patient is instructed to notify the clinician if the bleeding becomes excessive, a fever develops, or any other problems arise.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>If PiRAD 3, 4, or 5 lesions have been detected on MRI, the ultrasound biopsy technique can be combined with MRI using digital overlay, allowing for visualization in an MRI fusion biopsy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r216">[216]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r217">[217]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Biopsies of the MRI-targeted lesions should be performed first and labeled separately, followed by the standard sextant sampling.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r218">[218]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The patient is typically given a return appointment to review the biopsy results about 1 week later.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Procedural details of transperineal prostate biopsies are well described elsewhere.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r165">[165]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Transrectal Versus Transperineal Prostate Biopsies</bold>
</p>
        <p>The European Association of Urology has already recommended transperineal prostatic biopsies over transrectal biopsies in their 2021 Guidelines.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r91">[91]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r219">[219]</xref>&#x000a0;Both techniques use TRUS for imaging; however, the perineal approach typically uses a fixed external template to guide transperineal biopsies, although it can also be performed freehand. By avoiding transrectal biopsies and inoculating fecal material into the highly vascular prostate, post-procedure infectious complications are reduced from 5%&#x000a0;to 7% (urinary tract infections) and 1%&#x000a0;to 3% (sepsis) to 0.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r91">[91]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r220">[220]</xref>&#x000a0;Complications such as rectal bleeding, fever, and urinary retention are substantially reduced with transperineal biopsies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r221">[221]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r222">[222]</xref></p>
        <p>Transperineal prostate biopsies can now be performed freehand, with a brachytherapy perineal template grid, or&#x000a0;using robotic assistance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r223">[223]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r224">[224]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r225">[225]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r226">[226]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r227">[227]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r228">[228]</xref> These procedures can also be carried out under local anesthesia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r222">[222]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r223">[223]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r226">[226]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r228">[228]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r229">[229]</xref></p>
        <p>The cancer detection rate with the transperineal approach is equal to or somewhat higher, especially for anterior and apical prostatic malignancies. This approach also allows for longer biopsy cores, reduces procedural pain, and eliminates the routine need for prophylactic antibiotics, thereby improving antimicrobial stewardship and reducing the spread of bacterial resistance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r91">[91]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r230">[230]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r231">[231]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r232">[232]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r233">[233]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r234">[234]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r235">[235]</xref>&#x000a0;MRI fusion TRUS-guided biopsies may also be performed using the transperineal approach.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r235">[235]</xref> The benefits of the transperineal approach are clear, making it the preferred approach for all patients, especially those at a higher risk of infection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r91">[91]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r221">[221]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r236">[236]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The transperineal approach also enables image-guided prostate biopsies in patients without rectal access. In such cases, CT or MRI is used for planning, and biopsies are performed under fluoroscopy, with transperineal ultrasonography, or via a transgluteal approach using CT guidance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r237">[237]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r238">[238]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r239">[239]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r240">[240]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r241">[241]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r242">[242]</xref>&#x000a0;A prostate biopsy has also been performed using a transabdominal ultrasound-guided retropubic approach with CT scanning for needle tip positioning in a patient without rectal access.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r237">[237]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r243">[243]</xref></p>
        <p>Despite these clear advantages, significant challenges remain before this approach can be adopted as the standard of care in the United States and worldwide.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r236">[236]</xref> The transperineal approach takes more time, is more complex, and costs more than transrectal biopsies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r91">[91]</xref><ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/Keywords/UserViewPopup/178762#link_40514255">[144]</ext-link><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r236">[236]</xref>&#x000a0;There is also a steep learning curve for practitioners familiar only with the transrectal biopsy approach.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r236">[236]</xref>&#x000a0;Insurers in the United States reimburse the same regardless of approach, which is a significant disincentive to switch.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r91">[91]</xref> Implementation may also necessitate additional office equipment, different transducers, higher levels of anesthesia, disposable templates, and other supplies, which can pose challenges, especially in poorer regions worldwide.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r91">[91]</xref></p>
        <p>Transperitoneal prostate biopsies are becoming more prevalent worldwide, especially in Europe. Still, it remains unclear how long it will take for the United States and the rest of the world to fully adopt and transition to this approach.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r91">[91]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r236">[236]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Magnetic Resonance Imaging of&#x000a0;the Prostate</bold>
</p>
        <p>Multiparametric MRI of the prostate has proven to be the preferred modality for detecting suspicious or cancerous nodules in the prostate. When MRI images are used to guide biopsies of the prostate, the results are far superior to standard TRUS-guided sampling.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r141">[141]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r142">[142]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r244">[244]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r245">[245]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r246">[246]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r247">[247]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r248">[248]</xref></p>
        <p>Prostate MRI can identify extracapsular extension of prostatic lesions, possible involvement of the seminal vesicles, and determine the status of regional lymph nodes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r249">[249]</xref> MRI&#x000a0;can visualize otherwise undetectable abnormalities in unusual areas of the prostate where cancerous lesions are uncommon.&#x000a0;MRI is also used to assess metastases, which are often not well visualized on bone scans or CT scans, and to evaluate pathological fractures and associated complications.</p>
        <p>MRI<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>uses&#x000a0;pulsed radio waves in a powerful magnetic field to rapidly align and realign polarized molecules in tissues and organs, such as water. When the right radiofrequency is used, tissues release distinctive and characteristic radio waves, hence the resonance. Antennas pick up these tissue-generated radio signals, which can be used to create images.</p>
        <p>MRI uses a strong magnetic field to cause water molecules within organs and other bodily structures to align or line up.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r250">[250]</xref> This alignment is temporarily disrupted when the tissue is exposed to pulsed radio waves of the right frequency.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r250">[250]</xref>&#x000a0;In between the pulsed radio waves, when the signals are briefly turned off, the water molecules lose their alignment but quickly realign when the radio wave returns, emitting a distinctive radio signal. This tissue-generated signal is picked up by antennas in the MRI machine and used by a computer to generate an MRI image.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r250">[250]</xref></p>
        <p>No ionizing radiation is used, but metal and magnetic items can interfere. Additionally, the radio waves produced in the targeted organs are quite weak. Hence, MRI imaging requires a very strong magnetic field (3 Tesla MRI machines are typically recommended), and a lengthy exposure is necessary to obtain sufficient tissue-generated radiofrequency data to generate an image. The machines are noisy, but the procedure is painless, and hearing protection is provided to ensure patient comfort.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Key&#x000a0;Magnetic Resonance Imaging Diagnostic Parameters for&#x000a0;</bold>
<bold>Detecting Suspicious Prostatic Lesions</bold>
</p>
        <p>Urologists need to understand the significant characteristics and parameters of prostatic MRI imaging and diagnosis to better comprehend their patients' cancer risk and the relative anatomy of the lesion.</p>
        <p>
<bold>T1-weighted images:</bold> The radiofrequency pulses are optimized to highlight adipose tissue, making fat appear bright, white, or hyperintense. T1-weighted images are best used for visualizing more precise anatomical details, contrast-enhanced imaging, and detecting abnormalities in fatty tissues or lesions.</p>
        <p>
<bold>T2-weighted images:</bold> The radiofrequency timing is adjusted to highlight fat and water, which appear bright, white, or hyperintense.&#x000a0;T2-weighted images (T2WI) are best used to examine fluid accumulations, such as edema and cysts;&#x000a0;inflammatory lesions; and for soft tissue pathology, including neoplasms. T2WI is the standard sequence for prostate MRI, as prostate cancers tend to be hypoechoic on T2WI.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Apparent diffusion coefficient</bold>: This value reflects the relative resistance to the free diffusion of water molecules in tissues. The apparent diffusion coefficient (ADC) is automatically calculated from the different Diffusion-weighted imaging (DWI) signal intensities at varying gradient strengths.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r251">[251]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r252">[252]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r253">[253]</xref> Lower ADC values indicate restricted diffusion of water molecules, suggestive of tumors, whereas higher ADC values suggest free water diffusion, making cancer less likely.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r251">[251]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r252">[252]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r253">[253]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Lower ADC values indicate more restricted water diffusion and a higher likelihood of malignancy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r251">[251]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r252">[252]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r253">[253]</xref>&#x000a0;Typical ADC ranges for the prostate are as follows:<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r251">[251]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r252">[252]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r253">[253]</xref></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Normal prostate: ADC values typically range from 1.00 to 1.75 &#x000d7; 10<sup>&#x02212;3</sup> mm&#x000b2;/s with normal ADC mapping.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Suspicious for prostate cancer: ADC values&#x000a0;are lower than normal tissue, often &#x0003c;1.00&#x000a0;to 1.10 &#x000d7; 10<sup>&#x02212;3</sup> mm&#x000b2;/s with dark ADC mapping.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Suspicious for high-grade prostate cancer: ADC values are even lower, possibly &#x0003c;0.70 &#x000d7; 10<sup>&#x02212;3</sup> mm&#x000b2;/s with even darker ADC mapping.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Diffusion-weighted imaging:</bold> This reading represents the random movement of water molecules within the various zones of the prostate. Malignant tissue has increased cellularity, restricting free water movement compared to normal tissue or BPH.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r254">[254]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r255">[255]</xref> Lesions&#x000a0;are scored based on the diffusion-weighted imaging signal intensity compared to normal tissue within the same zone.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r254">[254]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r255">[255]</xref></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Normal prostate or BPH&#x000a0;appears dark on diffusion-weighted imaging and bright on ADC mapping, indicating relatively unrestricted free water diffusion.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Prostate cancer&#x000a0;typically exhibits a bright signal on diffusion-weighted imaging, accompanied by a dark image on ADC mapping, indicating focal areas of densely packed cells where water diffusion is restricted, which are more likely to be malignant.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Dynamic contrast-enhanced imaging</bold>: Immediately after the injection of contrast media (typically gadolinium, which changes the magnetic properties of nearby water molecules, making them clearer and brighter on MRI), multiple serial MRI images are collected.&#x000a0;Prostate cancers, with their enhanced vascularity, typically show faster and earlier tissue enhancement and quicker washout compared to normal tissue.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r256">[256]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r257">[257]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r258">[258]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r259">[259]</xref>&#x000a0;Good dynamic contrast-enhanced imaging requires rapid administration of the contrast bolus and quick image acquisition.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r256">[256]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r257">[257]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r258">[258]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r259">[259]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Prostate Imaging Reporting and Data System Classification</bold>
</p>
        <p>No single MRI parameter, value, or sequence is sufficient to diagnose prostate cancer; however, when considered together, they provide a very reliable estimation of possible malignancy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r260">[260]</xref>&#x000a0;The combined consideration of all these MRI parameters constitutes the Prostate Imaging Reporting and Data System (PI-RADS) score, a standardized classification system updated in 2015 and 2023 to the current industry standard PI-RADS version 2.1.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r260">[260]</xref>&#x000a0;The scoring is performed by radiology, providing a simple way to give urologists and other clinicians a quick, overall summary of the prostate MRI results in an understandable format.</p>
        <p>PI-RADS has 5 categories, from PI-RADS 1 to PI-RADS 5. These are defined according to the overall estimated risk of clinically significant prostate cancer as follows:<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r260">[260]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r261">[261]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>PI-RADS scoring:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>PI-RADS 1: Cancer risk is minimal&#x000a0;and&#x000a0;highly unlikely. Normal T2WI, diffusion-weighted imaging, and ADC mapping appear normal. There may be round, fully encapsulated nodules (BPH) in the transitional zone, but only uniform, homogeneous images are present in the peripheral zone.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r260">[260]</xref>&#x000a0;A biopsy is not recommended solely based on the PI-RADS score.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r260">[260]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r261">[261]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>PI-RADS 2: Cancer risk is low&#x000a0;and considered unlikely. Findings may include heterogeneity on T2WI and hypointensity of the peripheral zone on diffusion-weighted imaging. A mostly encapsulated or circumscribed nodule may exist without encapsulation (atypical nodule) in the transitional zone. Mild hypointense homogeneous imaging may be present between nodules. The peripheral zone&#x000a0;may have&#x000a0;linear or wedge-shaped hypointense or diffusely mild hypointense signal nodules, typically with indistinct margins.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r260">[260]</xref>&#x000a0;A biopsy is not recommended solely based on the PI-RADS score.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r260">[260]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r261">[261]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>PI-RADS 3: Cancer risk is moderate&#x000a0;and considered equivocal. Findings may include heterogeneous lesions with indistinct margins on T2WI. Nodules appear hyperintense on diffusion-weighted imaging and hypointense on ADC, with focal areas of mild-to-moderate hypointensity on ADC or mild hyperintensity&#x000a0;on diffusion-weighted imaging. A lesion may exhibit either significant hypointensity on ADC or significant hyperintensity on diffusion-weighted imaging, but not both. The lesion does not meet the criteria for any other PI-RADS category.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r260">[260]</xref>&#x000a0;A biopsy is recommended based on the PI-RADS score.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r260">[260]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r261">[261]</xref>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>For peripheral zone lesions otherwise classified as PI-RADS 3, positive findings on dynamic contrast enhancement upgrade the classification to PI-RADS 4.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r260">[260]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Positive findings on dynamic contrast enhancement include early or simultaneous enhancement of adjacent normal prostatic tissues corresponding to the suspicious finding on T2WI or diffusion-weighted imaging.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r260">[260]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r262">[262]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Early contrast washout is also a positive finding.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r260">[260]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Considering the PSA density value can help avoid unnecessary biopsies safely in this equivocal PI-RADS classification.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r133">[133]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r263">[263]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r264">[264]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r265">[265]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r266">[266]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r267">[267]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>PI-RADS 4: Cancer risk is high and considered likely. A nodule may be a lenticular or non-circumscribed lesion less than 1.5 cm in maximum size in the transitional zone or a circumscribed nodule less than 1.5 cm in the peripheral zone.&#x000a0;Lesions are&#x000a0;moderately homogeneous and hypointense on T2WI, hyperintense on diffusion-weighted imaging, and hypointense on ADC.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r260">[260]</xref>&#x000a0;A biopsy is recommended based on the PI-RADS score.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r260">[260]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r261">[261]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>PI-RADS 5: Cancer risk is high&#x000a0;and considered extremely likely. Criteria are the same as for PI-RADS 4, but the lesion is 1.5 cm or larger, and there is evidence of extra-prostatic extension.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r260">[260]</xref>&#x000a0;A biopsy is recommended based on the PI-RADS score.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r260">[260]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r261">[261]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>MRI imaging is best used when the decision to do a biopsy has already been made after reviewing the patient's life expectancy, age, expectations, comorbidities, surgical risks, potential benefits, treatment options, and the patient's willingness to accept therapy if a significant cancer were found.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r268">[268]</xref>&#x000a0;Various strategies to improve the decision-making process regarding prostatic biopsies before performing an MRI include bioassay risk stratification tests (designed to identify low-risk patients unlikely to benefit from a prostate biopsy), PSA-density determinations, and the use of age-adjusted PSA values.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r134">[134]</xref></p>
        <p>As a general rule, PI-RADS 3, 4, and 5 lesions are biopsied using MRI fusion-guided techniques, whereas a standard TRUS-guided procedure is used for PI-RADS 1 and 2 lesions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r269">[269]</xref>&#x000a0;Some recent studies suggest that PI-RADS 3 lesions may not require targeted MRI fusion-guided biopsies, as the incidence of clinically significant prostate cancer detected in these specified areas is quite small, and a standard TRUS-guided procedure is sufficient.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r270">[270]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r271">[271]</xref>&#x000a0;Biopsies may be&#x000a0;omitted in patients with PI-RADS 3 lesions who have a PSA density (PSA/prostate volume cc) below 0.15, which could prevent up to 48% of unnecessary procedures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r272">[272]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r273">[273]</xref></p>
        <p>Prostate malignancies are most often found in the peripheral zone. Typical imaging features include T2WI hypointensity (hypoechoic), low ADC values (dark, hypointense), high diffusion-weighted imaging readings (bright, hyperintense), and positive findings on dynamic contrast-enhanced imaging. The PI-RADS imaging score categorizes these findings into a single, easily understood score. Patients with PI-RADS 3, 4, or 5 lesions should generally have an MRI fusion-guided prostate biopsy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r260">[260]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Magnetic Resonance Imaging Fusion-Guided Prostate Biopsies</bold>
</p>
        <p>When MRI imaging shows suspicious (PI-RADS 3, 4, or 5) lesions, the radiologist electronically outlines and digitally colors them. Although biopsies can be performed directly in the MRI machine itself, most often an MRI fusion-guided TRUS biopsy is performed. With its suspicious areas marked, outlined, and colored by the radiologist, the MRI image is digitally superimposed, fused, and correlated with the live prostatic TRUS.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r216">[216]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r217">[217]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r218">[218]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r274">[274]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r275">[275]</xref></p>
        <p>This MRI fusion-guided technique enables the biopsy to be performed in the same manner as the standard biopsy procedure described above, with the added benefit that suspicious lesions identified on the MRI are now visible and highlighted on the TRUS image for targeted core sampling.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r216">[216]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r217">[217]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r218">[218]</xref></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>The digital MRI image, with its colored target lesions, can be electronically superimposed, overlaid, and correlated with the real-time transrectal prostatic ultrasound picture, making the suspicious areas from the MRI visible during TRUS.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>With the MRI correlated and superimposed on the live ultrasound image, directed biopsies of the suspicious lesions can now be performed using TRUS.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>At least 2, but typically&#x000a0;3 or 4, samples are taken from every suspicious lesion identified by MRI.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Typically, a standard 12-core sextant biopsy sampling is performed in addition to biopsies of the suspicious lesions identified on the MRI.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r218">[218]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Micro-ultrasound technology: </bold>This technique, using an extremely high-frequency transducer (29 MHz), provides exceptional resolution and detail (3 times more than standard TRUS), enabling the visualization of lesions as small as 70 &#x000b5;m.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r276">[276]</xref> Preliminary results suggest that micro-ultrasound-guided biopsies can identify prostatic malignancies that are not detected on standard TRUS or prostatic MRI, yielding results comparable to those of systemic biopsies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r276">[276]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r277">[277]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r278">[278]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r279">[279]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r280">[280]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r281">[281]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r282">[282]</xref></p>
        <p>Micro-ultrasonography is a promising improvement over conventional transrectal prostatic ultrasound.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r276">[276]</xref>&#x000a0;This technique could eventually replace systemic biopsies in clinical practice, as it reduces the diagnosis of clinically insignificant, indolent cancers while increasing the detection rate of treatable prostatic malignancies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r283">[283]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Shear wave elastography: </bold>This technique has similar sensitivity and specificity for prostate cancer detection as MRI.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r284">[284]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r285">[285]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r286">[286]</xref>&#x000a0;Prostate cancers tend to be harder and denser than normal tissue. Elastography can measure the increased firmness and stiffness of malignant prostatic tissue with good diagnostic results, similar to prostatic MRI.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r284">[284]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r285">[285]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r286">[286]</xref>&#x000a0;Although multiparametric prostatic MRI has become the standard of care for screening patients with elevated PSA levels and possible prostate cancer, elastography may still play a useful role in equivocal or borderline cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r287">[287]</xref></p>
        <p>Combining several ultrasound imaging modalities, such as elastography with contrast-enhanced ultrasonography or MRI, yields superior diagnostic results compared to using either method alone.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r98">[98]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r99">[99]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r288">[288]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r289">[289]</xref>&#x000a0;For example, the combination of multiparametric prostate MRI and shear wave elastography yields a reported overall sensitivity of nearly 95% and a specificity of 96%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r289">[289]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-129473.s10" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>There are no known complications from transrectal prostate ultrasonography. However, there are potential complications if the ultrasound is performed in conjunction with a transrectal prostate biopsy, such as hematospermia (93%), hematuria (65%), rectal bleeding (37%), erectile dysfunction (34%), urinary tract infection (15%), urinary retention (6%), and sepsis (1%).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r46">[46]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r290">[290]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r291">[291]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r292">[292]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r293">[293]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r294">[294]</xref>&#x000a0;Although these are encountered relatively frequently, they are typically mild and self-limited, except for sepsis, which is uncommon.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r291">[291]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r292">[292]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r293">[293]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r295">[295]</xref>&#x000a0;Other complications include epididymitis, orchitis, pain, and prostatitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r46">[46]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r290">[290]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r291">[291]</xref></p>
        <p>The incidence of these complications tends to rise as the number of biopsy samples increases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r292">[292]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r296">[296]</xref></p>
        <p>Differentiating prostate cancer from BPH&#x000a0;solely based on ultrasonographic findings can be quite challenging,&#x000a0;especially if the malignant pathology is located anteriorly, in the central zone, or in the transitional zone.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r292">[292]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r297">[297]</xref>&#x000a0;In such cases, multiparametric prostatic MRI is suggested to optimize targeting. If no suspicious lesions are detected on MRI, proceed with standard TRUS-guided sextant biopsies if there is sufficient suspicion based on clinical parameters, history, or laboratory results.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r134">[134]</xref></p>
        <p>Erectile dysfunction&#x000a0;develops in about one-third of patients after transrectal prostate biopsies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r292">[292]</xref>&#x000a0;Patients who develop such erectile dysfunction typically recover spontaneously in about 1 to 3 months, but resolution may take up to 6 months in some cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r292">[292]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r293">[293]</xref></p>
        <p>The incidence of urinary retention is associated with the number of biopsy cores taken and the prostatic volume.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r292">[292]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r293">[293]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r298">[298]</xref>&#x000a0;This type of urinary retention is typically transitory but may require a temporary Foley catheter.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r293">[293]</xref></p>
        <p>The increasing rate of drug-resistant urinary tract infections with antibiotic-resistant bacteria after transrectal prostate biopsies is a major global concern.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r62">[62]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r299">[299]</xref>&#x000a0;This risk can be reduced by judicious and appropriate antibiotic prophylaxis, rectal antisepsis immediately before the biopsy, targeted antimicrobial prophylaxis based on rectal swab cultures, and transitioning to transperineal biopsies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r91">[91]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r292">[292]</xref></p>
        <p>The incidence of urinary retention&#x000a0;is associated with the number of biopsy cores taken and the prostatic volume.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r292">[292]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r293">[293]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r298">[298]</xref>&#x000a0;This type of urinary retention is typically transitory but may require a temporary Foley catheter.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r293">[293]</xref></p>
        <p>Pain management&#x000a0;is a significant issue in performing transrectal biopsies. Careful probe insertion, use of pre-biopsy rectal analgesics, adequate lubrication, gentle handling to avoid excessive manipulation, effective periprostatic and apical anesthetic blocks, and allowing sufficient time for anesthesia to take effect are key measures for minimizing patient pain and discomfort.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r292">[292]</xref>&#x000a0;This pain can last for several hours after the procedure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r292">[292]</xref>&#x000a0;Risk factors for increased pain include the number of biopsy samples taken, prostatic volume, and younger age.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r292">[292]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r294">[294]</xref>&#x000a0;Combination anesthesia protocols have demonstrated superior pain relief compared to single modality analgesia and are therefore recommended.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r170">[170]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r292">[292]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r300">[300]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r301">[301]</xref></p>
        <p>Periprostatic nerve blocks may rarely cause vasovagal syncope.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r156">[156]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r170">[170]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r172">[172]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r292">[292]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r302">[302]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r303">[303]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r304">[304]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r305">[305]</xref></p>
        <p>Rectal pathology, such as&#x000a0;hemorrhoids and anal fissures, can interfere with TRUS-guided biopsies or increase the risk of complications. As prostate biopsies are not emergency procedures, rectal conditions should be treated beforehand whenever possible.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r292">[292]</xref> Alternatively, patients may be converted to a transperineal biopsy approach, although TRUS is still required.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r292">[292]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Significant rectal bleeding&#x000a0;is uncommon, and massive bleeding is rare. Treatment may include an endoscopic rectal examination (rectoscopy)&#x000a0; for diagnosis and immediate therapy. This treatment may include endoscopic ligation, epinephrine injection, rectal balloon placement, or sclerotherapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r292">[292]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-129473.r294">[294]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-129473.s11" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>TRUS of the prostate is an&#x000a0;essential tool in the evaluation of prostatic pathology. Understanding the techniques enables the ordering of appropriate tests for the correct scenarios, potentially in situations that may not have been recommended in the past. Advancing techniques also enable better diagnosis and reduce the need for cross-sectional CT imaging, which involves exposure to ionizing radiation.</p>
        <p>Given the increasing incidence and burden of prostate cancer, infection, and BPH on the healthcare system, inexpensive diagnostic tools such as TRUS that provide a prompt diagnosis and are easy to perform will become even more critical.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Understanding the appropriate options for prophylaxis and anesthesia, as well as knowing the proper technique for TRUS-guided prostate biopsies, is essential for modern medical practice.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-129473.s12" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>The relationships among the sonographer, radiologist, and ordering physician&#x02014;typically a urologist or primary care clinician&#x02014;are crucial for optimizing patient-centered care. As with any diagnostic data, interpretation and subsequent action will require the efforts of an interprofessional healthcare team, including clinicians, mid-level practitioners, nurses, radiologists, and ultrasound techs, all working collaboratively and sharing information openly to drive optimal patient outcomes.</p>
        <p>A good working relationship between the radiologist and ordering physician is key to having an open dialogue about the indications for and the extent of TRUS's and prostatic MRI's diagnostic capabilities, ensuring that testing is ordered correctly. Communication should increase accordingly as new technologies emerge and become available. The need for increased communication is critical, as equipment capabilities vary between countries and institutions. Incorporating new technologies can improve patient care by providing additional, more accurate information.</p>
        <p>The relationship between the radiologist and sonographer is equally essential. As the radiologist is limited by the sonographer's images, a common understanding of the technique is crucial to avoid missed or inaccurate diagnoses.</p>
        <p>The radiologist should make every effort to indicate the relative locations and sizes of all suspicious nodules, especially at the apex, to evaluate possible involvement of the neurovascular bundles (as this has clinical significance for possible nerve-sparing surgeries), and any evidence of seminal vesicle involvement or extracapsular spread.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Close cooperation, effective communication, and mutual understanding between radiology and urology are essential for making optimal medical decisions and achieving improved outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-129473.s13">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
